    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d668">

                <pb facs="1364" n="1352" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg1352"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Walter S. Robertson to General
                    Marshall</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-07-13">13 July 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5378. For your information the pertinent parts of Generalissimo’s order of 1 July
                    as cited in your 1077<anchor n="15" xml:id="d668-f1"/> and 1087<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d668-f2"/> have been transmitted <pb facs="1365" n="1353"
                        xml:id="pg1353"/> to all field team chairmen, as well as the information
                    contained in your 1084.<anchor n="17" xml:id="d668-f3"/> Senior field team
                    chairmen have been instructed to determine when orders of Generalissimo and
                    General Chou En-lai are in the hands of subordinate commanders within reach of
                    our field teams. In no case were the contents nor the gist of the contents of
                    Mao Tse-tung’s orders learned by US members of field teams. US Branch Executive
                    Headquarters likewise without information on contents of order.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
